{"claims": "Claim A (source: EV-CW-29338B-f53324e5, time: 01:10): She heard a van pull in at the bottom of the lane

Claim B (source: EV-CW-29338B-9b508c30, time: 01:39): A light-coloured panel van with no visible markings stops at the lane's far (south) end

ENTITY-RESOLUTION CONTEXT: both claims have already been independently resolved to the same canonical entity/location: "bottom of the lane". Treat this as an established fact.", "entity_context": "ENTITY-RESOLUTION CONTEXT: both claims have already been independently resolved to the same canonical entity/location: "bottom of the lane". Treat this as an established fact.", "prosecutor_argument": "Both claims describe the same type of event — a van arriving/pulling in and stopping — at the same canonically resolved location, the "bottom of the lane" (the lane's far south end), so under the rubric they default to one real-world event. No third piece of evidence independently confirms two separate van arrivals, therefore the 29-minute gap is not grounds to split them into different events but is a genuine timing conflict: the van is heard pulling in at 01:10 in Claim A, yet the light-coloured panel van associated with person with the tool is only recorded stopping there at 01:39 in Claim B. Both timestamps cannot be correct for the same stop, making this a flaggable discrepancy in the van's arrival time.", "prosecutor_proposed_question": "Review continuous CCTV, audio, or other logs for 01:00-01:45 to determine whether there was one or two van arrivals at the bottom of the lane, and verify the clock accuracy/synchronisation of the 01:10 and 01:39 sources.", "defender_argument": "No real counter-argument exists under the rubric. Both claims describe a van arriving/stopping at the same resolved location, 'bottom of the lane,' and there is no evidence of a second distinct van arrival or a documented clock offset to explain the 29-minute difference. That gap alone cannot justify treating them as different events, so the timing discrepancy stands as a genuine conflict.", "judge_relationship": "conflicts", "judge_reason": "The Prosecutor's argument was more evidence-grounded because both claims describe a van arriving and stopping at the same resolved location, the bottom of the lane, with no evidence of a second distinct arrival or a documented clock offset to explain the 29-minute gap.", "judge_verification_question": "Was there one or two van arrivals at the bottom of the lane between 01:00-01:45 on continuous CCTV, audio, or other logs, and what is the verified clock accuracy and synchronisation of the 01:10 and 01:39 sources?"}

{"claims": "Claim A (source: EV-CW-29338B-9b508c30, time: 01:42): The window moves inward

Claim B (source: EV-CW-29338B-805c506b, time: 01:56): WINDOW-STK-W1 recorded window_state_open with state alarm in ZONE-3 at 2026-03-12T01:56:12

ENTITY-RESOLUTION CONTEXT: both claims have already been independently resolved to the same canonical entity/location: "side window". Treat this as an established fact.", "entity_context": "ENTITY-RESOLUTION CONTEXT: both claims have already been independently resolved to the same canonical entity/location: "side window". Treat this as an established fact.", "prosecutor_argument": "Both claims describe the same type of event — a side window opening/moving — and entity-resolution has already established they resolve to the same canonical 'side window,' with no third evidence of two separate openings, so under the rubric they must be treated as one real-world event. The conflict is a material 14-minute timing and state discrepancy: Claim A places inward window movement at 01:42, while WINDOW-STK-W1 in Claim B does not record window_state_open/alarm for person with the tool in ZONE-3 until 2026-03-12T01:56:12, implying either the window moved well before the alarm triggered or one timestamp is wrong. That gap cannot be dismissed as two events without explicit evidence of a second opening, and no known clock offset is documented to explain it as support.", "prosecutor_proposed_question": "Can investigators pull WINDOW-STK-W1's raw state-change and clock-sync logs for 01:35-02:00 and any video of the side window to determine whether there was one opening at 01:42 with a delayed 01:56:12 alarm, or two distinct openings?", "defender_argument": "No real counter-argument exists under the rubric: both claims concern the same resolved 'side window' moving/opening, and there is no evidence of a second distinct opening, a documented clock offset, or a sensor delay explaining the 01:42 versus 2026-03-12T01:56:12 gap. Treating the inward movement as a separate pre-opening phase would be speculative, as nothing in the claims distinguishes it from the window_state_open event in Claim B. The 14-minute discrepancy must therefore be treated as a timing conflict for the same event.", "judge_relationship": "conflicts", "judge_reason": "The Prosecutor's argument was more evidence-grounded because both claims describe a side window opening at the same resolved location with a 14-minute timing difference and no evidence of a second distinct opening or a documented clock offset", "judge_verification_question": "Can investigators pull WINDOW-STK-W1's raw state-change and clock-sync logs for 01:35-02:00 and any video of the side window to determine whether there was one opening at 01:42 with a delayed 01:56:12 alarm, or two distinct openings?"}

{"claims": "Claim A (source: EV-CW-29338B-dd77ad72, time: 18:00): Tobias told Halloran he locked the customer door from outside at about six in the evening

Claim B (source: EV-CW-29338B-6627e98c, time: 18:02): READER-CUSTOMER-DOOR recorded door_locked_from_outside with card K02, result ok

ENTITY-RESOLUTION CONTEXT: both claims have already been independently resolved to the same canonical entity/location: "customer door". Treat this as an established fact.", "entity_context": "ENTITY-RESOLUTION CONTEXT: both claims have already been independently resolved to the same canonical entity/location: "customer door". Treat this as an established fact.", "prosecutor_argument": "Both claims describe the identical event type — locking the customer door from outside — at the same resolved entity 'customer door', at about 18:00 versus 18:02, so under the rubric they describe ONE real-world event with no evidence of a second separate locking. The genuine conflict is actor attribution: Tobias says he personally locked it, while READER-CUSTOMER-DOOR records the successful lock as person with the tool using card K02. That identity discrepancy, not the negligible 2-minute timing difference, is a flaggable conflict suggesting card-sharing, misattribution, or a false account.", "prosecutor_proposed_question": "Who physically presented card K02 at the customer door at 18:02 — check CCTV and card-custody/assignment records to determine whether Tobias used person with the tool's card, or person with the tool was the actual actor contrary to Tobias's statement?", "defender_argument": "No real counter-argument exists on this evidence. Both claims describe locking the customer door from outside at essentially the same time, about 18:00 and 18:02, at the same resolved location, so they refer to one event. Nothing in the claims shows a second locking, a clock offset, or that Tobias was using person with the tool's card K02, so the Tobias-versus-person with the tool actor discrepancy remains a genuine conflict.", "judge_relationship": "conflicts", "judge_reason": "The Prosecutor's argument is more evidence-grounded because both claims describe locking the customer door from outside at the same resolved location only two minutes apart with no evidence of a second locking, yet attribute the act to Tobias versus person with the tool via card K02.", "judge_verification_question": "Who physically presented card K02 at the customer door at 18:02 — check CCTV and card-custody/assignment records to determine whether Tobias used person with the tool's card, or person with the tool was the actual actor contrary to Tobias's statement?"}
